I"9<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>Rare diseases are individually uncommon, but together they affect a lot of people. We know that COVID-19 hit people with rare diseases especially hard. What we did not know is how that burden differs between ethnic groups, because we did not have a clear picture of who has which rare disease in the first place.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>Working with national health records covering 62.5 million people in England, we counted how common 406 rare diseases are within different, finely defined ethnic groups. We then looked at how much COVID-19 infection each of these groups of patients carried.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>Rare diseases are not spread evenly across ethnic groups, and the pattern of COVID-19 risk by ethnicity among people with rare diseases is complicated. It often runs against the trends seen in the general population. This is likely because the underlying differences in which rare diseases are common in which groups run deep.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>A map like this shows which combinations of rare disease and ethnic group face the greatest overlapping risk. That helps health services decide where to look first, and it gives researchers a foundation for explaining why those gaps exist.</p>
